16722-   "witness": "probe:regress/sh_pclock_probe.py (rung 3, 16 rows, SHIPPED). MEASURED 6/16 on the true pre-fix build and 16/16 post-fix, SAME SCRIPT BOTH SIDES. The pre-fix build was made from `git show HEAD:src/devices/dev_sh4.c` into a private clone and grep-confirmed to still contain `INTERNAL ERROR: pclock`, NOT from build/ -- a seat measured build/gxemul's mtime moving underneath it mid-review, so a run it believed was pre-fix was post-fix and nothing in the output distinguished them. Controls: LIVENESS (guest store then guest load of an asymmetric 0x12345678) and DEVICE SIGNATURE (IPRD reset value 0xda74, which RAM cannot produce and an unmapped address answers with `non-existant paddr`), both green on both sides and both in their OWN sessions so neither touches TCR.",
16723-   "closure": {
16724-    "by": "fable5-mainloop",
16725-    "badge": "A",
16726:    "note": "*** THE DEFECT IS A CLASS, AND THE FIX IS AT THE GUARD FOR THAT REASON. *** cpu_sh.c:233-234 adds the sh4 device from `arch == 4`, so THE CPU BRINGS THE TMU AND THE BOARD BRINGS THE CLOCK, and nothing checked that the two agree. Five registered SH machine types name an arch-4 CPU; only machine_dreamcast.c:51 and machine_landisk.c:81 assign pclock. Fixing the three machine files would leave every FUTURE arch-4 machine inheriting the same host kill.\nTHE ROW'S TITLE SAID 'hpcsh AND testsh' AND THAT UNDERCOUNTS. Two pass-1 seats independently found that machine_test.c:535 is MACHINE_DEFAULT_CPU(baresh) and :541 is MACHINE_DEFAULT_CPU(testsh) -- two separately registered machines in one file -- so `baresh` was never named by the brief or the filing. Measured: it dies identically. THREE machine types, not two. A seat also measured -e jornada690 reproducing (the subtype is never read; same code path) and confirmed all five machines carry the device.\nTHE FIX follows #447's precedent twenty lines below in the same arm, and its stated reason: 'substituting a prescaler for a clock source we cannot provide would be inventing a rate'. Once-only diagnostic, break, no state touched, using the ELEVENTH SH4_VAL_* latch class -- free, because the tenth-class enum conversion was already discharged. The guard STAYS ABOVE the prescaler switch: run it lower and timer_hz is already set from `idata & 3`, so with pclock == 0 the debug() line announces 'clock set to 0.000000 Hz'. `m_below` measures exactly that and is caught by `unlatch` alone.\nDE-ESCALATION CLAUSE DISCHARGED BY MEASURED KILL. The predicate that gated death is exactly one, and a seat established it was THE LAST GUEST-REACHABLE exit( IN THE DEVICE (one wrapped in #ifdef SH4_DEBUG, three on interrupt_handler_lookup construction failures; LINE NUMBERS OMITTED DELIBERATELY -- an earlier draft cited four and the paragraph correcting the false panel claim shifted every one of them, which is the finding: a comment citing line numbers in its own file invalidates them when edited). Four accept rows across TWO machines and TWO channels each -- a single row pinned at landisk's 33.33 MHz is passed by a mutant that HARDCODES 33333333, and dreamcast's 50 MHz is the discriminator. They redden under a guard-only growth mutant `|| (pclock % 256) != 0`; guard-only is the correct form by the #451 discriminator because THE LATCH OPERAND IS THE CONSTANT 1, which the guard cannot feed.\nTHE HONEST COST HAS TWO HALVES, and the round demoted the first too far before two seats corrected it. (a) The STALL remains the primary behavioural cost, and for automation it turns an immediate nonzero exit into a possible timeout. (b) ADDITIONALLY the diagnostic degrades from unmissable to once-per-timer. (b) does not replace (a). What narrows (a)'s practical reach today: hpcsh's MACHINE_SETUP has its hpc_bootinfo half commented out at :48-64 -- the live body still registers RAM mirrors and a framebuffer -- so with no console wiring, no bootinfo and no disk NO OS BOOTS THERE. Note the stall is only hypothetical ON HPCSH: baresh and testsh run hand-written test programs, and a program that programs TCR and polls TCNT IS the hang. This detector plants exactly such a program; what actually degrades is the diagnostic, from unmissable to once-per-timer-for-the-device-lifetime.\n*** THE ROUND SHIPPED A FALSE CLAIM ABOUT ITS OWN PANEL AND THE MISCOUNTED SEAT CAUGHT IT. *** An early draft of the shipped comment said 'a panel of nine seats reached this 8-1'; it was written WHILE THE NINTH SEAT WAS STILL RUNNING and that seat then did not vote B. Corrected in place. Accurate: all nine answered, ALL NINE AGREE THE DECLINE IS MANDATORY, seven say it is the whole fix, two would additionally give the SYNTHETIC machines a defined pclock -- filed as sh4testclock, not merged, per the round-scope rule.\nCORRECTED AFTER THE CODE COMMIT, by pass 2: the guard-placement claim cited #447's silent-wrong-rate hazard, which CANNOT OCCUR AT THIS PREDICATE -- pclock == 0 makes every prescaler case 0.0 and timer_hz[] is already 0.0 from the DEVINIT memset. The placement is still right, for diagnostic consistency (the debug() line is unlatched and would print per-STORE). And `m_below` was misattributed to that claim: it is the missing-`break` mutant caught by `unlatch`, and NOTHING in the kill table measures the guard's position. Also: the read-back channel is itself a FABRICATION -- real SH-4 always latches TCR, so a guest doing read-modify-write silently loses its update, and `unlatch` pins that as correct. It is the lesser of two fabrications, not an architectural channel, and the record now says so."
