18532-     "note": "PASS 1, nine seats. *** MY PREMISE 1 WAS REFUTED BY GIT, NOT MERELY OVER-READ. *** The brief claimed Site A is 'already half-fixed' because the read branch's exit(1) is commented out while the write branch's is live, and inferred someone had decided the kill was wrong. `git log -L 676,700:src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` returns EXACTLY ONE COMMIT -- the unmodified upstream import. This fork never touched those lines. The true explanation is a BETTER argument: GDROM_STAT three lines later has its commented-out exit in the OPPOSITE (write) branch, so it is a REACHABILITY TRACE -- upstream commented out whichever exit he happened to hit against one driver. The live kill survived because that driver never wrote a non-0xb value, not because such writes are unthinkable. Three seats, three levels: one accepted the premise, one called the inference weak, one ran git log -L. PREMISE 5 ALSO WRONG: removing these guards does NOT delete the announcement -- fatal() remains and keeps logging; only process termination goes. The de-escalation cost is therefore lower than #447/#462, where the fix converted a loud kill into a SILENTLY refused write. DETECTOR TRAPS: do NOT pin d->stat (reading +0x8c calls update_stat which overwrites it unconditionally -- born vacuous); and every accept row here asserts ABSENCE of a diagnostic at a warn-once site, so a decline row running earlier in the same session sets the latch, suppresses the growth mutant's diagnostic, and leaves the accept row GREEN under its own mutant -- each accept row needs its OWN session, which cuts against the probe's one-session rule and the round must say which rule governs which row. SITE A HAS NO STATE CHANNEL: its write branch contains no store even for the legal 0xb, so only an absence-of-diagnostic row is available. PREREQUISITE: gdromwarnbits' pre-set trigger FIRES HERE -- 6 of 8 bits used, uint8_t, and this round needs 3 more (Site A's read branch is an unlatched flood today). 1u<<8 truncates to 0 and the diagnostic floods, so the enum-of-bit-positions conversion is due IN-ROUND, not filed."
