                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "fable5",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "Held out of #459 as a CAPABILITY change in a different file and a different semantic class, per the round-scope rule -- two seats proposed it and two refused the merge. THE ARGUMENT FOR: baresh and testsh are SYNTHETIC machines, so their parameters are the project\u0027s own definition rather than a hardware fact; assigning a pclock invents nothing, and it is the only way the tree gets a machine on which a future TMU round can build a witness at all. THE ARGUMENT AGAINST: testsh\u0027s value today is precisely as the regression case for \u0027an arch-4 CPU whose board omitted pclock\u0027, and giving it a clock deletes the only test of the missing-clock path -- so any round doing this MUST keep one machine clockless (baresh or hpcsh) and say which. A seat MEASURED 2^25 = 33554432 working, chosen because every TMU prescaler (/4, /16, /64, /256) divides it exactly and nobody will mistake it for a Jornada measurement; it produces 8388608.0 Hz at /4."
                        }
                    ]
    },
    {
        "id":  "sh4cpudevscope",
        "title":  "sh4cpudevscope  the SH-4 on-chip block is added PER-CPU but is machine-scoped, and sh4pclock is one symptom of three",
        "state":  "open",
        "commits":  "",
        "hold":  "",
        "witness":  "none",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  ""
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "fable5",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "*** THE CLASS #459 IS AN INSTANCE OF. *** cpu_sh.c:233-234 does device_add(machine, \"sh4\") from inside the CPU constructor, so a machine-scoped device is created once per CPU. Three measured symptoms: (1) sh4pclock -- the CPU brings the TMU and the board brings the clock, with nothing checking they agree, CLOSED in #459 at the guard; (2) `-n 2 -E landisk` REFUSES TO CONSTRUCT with `ERROR! \"sh4\" collides with device 11 (\"sh4\")`, so SMP is impossible on every SH machine and the reason is this line; (3) dev_sh4.c (near the end of devinit) hardcodes `machine[0].cpu[0].irq[...]` for the TMU interrupt lookup, which is only correct because (2) makes a second CPU impossible. Fixing (2) without (3) would produce a device on CPU 1 whose timer interrupts route to CPU 0."
                        }
                    ]
    },
    {
        "id":  "sh4pclockquiet",
        "title":  "sh4pclockquiet  five measured mutants of #459\u0027s pclock guard all score 16/16, because the declining-write value set is exactly {0x0000, 0x0001}",
        "state":  "open",
        "commits":  "",
        "hold":  "",
        "witness":  "none",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  ""
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "COVERAGE DEBT filed after #459\u0027s capped ask-again. A measuring seat built six mutants and FIVE scored 16/16: m_unie (`\u0026\u0026 !(idata \u0026 TCR_UNIE)`) which lands a TCR with timer_hz == 0 for EXACTLY the value a guest must write to use a TMU at all, while going QUIETER than the fix; m_below_switch (guard moved below the debug line) giving 4 unlatched `clock set to 0.000000 Hz` announcements for 4 stores; m_valkey (key `idata ? idata : 1u`) giving 3 lines for 4 values; m_tmr2 (`\u0026\u0026 timer_nr != 2`); and m_wrongnr (every line says \u0027timer 0\u0027). Only m_valnarrow died, 15/16, killed by `unlatch`. *** THE ROOT CAUSE IS ONE SENTENCE: the declining-write value set is exactly {0x0000, 0x0001} -- the three kill rows and says/once/pertmr all write 0x0000 and unlatch writes TCR_TPSC_P16, so ANY narrowing predicate that admits both escapes. *** m_valnarrow\u0027s death shows the remedy: a second distinct value. FOUR of the five closing rows cost ZERO extra sessions; only unlatch2 at TCR_UNIE|TCR_TPSC_P4 needs a new one, and it kills m_unie and the whole narrowing family."
                        }
                    ]
    },
    {
        "id":  "gdromkeyindex",
        "state":  "open",
        "commits":  "",
        "hold":  "",
        "witness":  "none needed -- MEASURED detector escapes on shipped, above-floor material, i.e. coverage debt. The six closing rows were validated at 38/38 by the measuring seat, but the DIFF ITSELF IS GONE (its session scratchpad); the design is recorded in the entry and is sufficient to rebuild",
        "title":  "gdromkeyindex  five mutants score a full 32/32 against #461\u0027s detector, and one of them writes the DMA arming registers from the CONTROL window",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  ""
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "opus5",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-23",
                            "note":  "18 builds, 20 probe runs, against source byte-identical to ba30e64. FIVE new 32/32 escapes. FILED not fixed: the stopping rule reserves in-round fixes for a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and #461\u0027s detector is above the vacuity floor on all four clauses -- so these are coverage debt, the #447 disposition. *** E1 IS THE FINDING, AND IT DEFEATS THE ROUND\u0027S STRUCTURAL DEFENCE BY GOING ROUND IT. *** Exchange the word- and bit-index expressions in gdrom_unimpl_first (`bits[k \u003e\u003e 5]` / `1u \u003c\u003c (k \u0026 31)` transposed). MEASURED, twice, with a standalone probe: four DECLINED 32-bit READS in the CONTROL window write 1 into dma_reg[1] (destination), [2] (length), [3] (count) and [5] (enable) -- which is EXACTLY case 0x18\u0027s arming condition, reached from the wrong window entirely. bits is a uint32_t[2]; `bits[k \u0026 31]` runs to index 31 and the struct puts dma_reg[0] six words past it. ASan is blind (member-to-member inside one malloc). *** THE BOUNDS GUARD STILL PASSES: k is still off/4 \u003c nkeys. The round\u0027s defence protects the BOUND; E1 lives in the INDEX, and no row was on the index. *** Honest scope from the seat: it is one transposition, and each HALF dies alone (m_swapidx 31/32, m_swapbit 29/32) -- the rows measure key DISTINCTNESS, which a transposition preserves exactly, so the detector catches either half and misses the whole. E2 `|=` -\u003e `=` (32/32): defeats once-per-offset on any REVISIT -- read +0x00, +0x04, +0x00 gives three lines, so a repeated register scan re-floods forever. Every existing row is blind because none revisits a word after touching another in the same bitmap word. E3 the alignment guard rewritten as `len != sizeof(uint32_t)` (32/32): re-opens the alias, MEASURED -- a 4-byte write at DMA+0x05 lands 0xffffffff in dma_reg[1] against the seeded 0x11223344. Every existing guard row uses a NARROW access, so width and alignment are indistinguishable to the detector. No SH4 instruction can issue a misaligned 32-bit store, but memory_rw can, and debugger_cmds.c:1353 is one caller. E4 both alignment ternaries in the DMA diagnostics inverted (32/32) -- the messages lie about dropped-vs-stored and no row reads the text. E5 `if (idata != 0)` -\u003e `if (0)` at case 0x18 (32/32): the DMA arm can be present, correctly routed, and completely INERT. Outside #461\u0027s semantic class -- it belongs to whoever fixes gdromexitsix\u0027s DMA+0x18 pair, and it is the same gap kimi named as its M2. SIX CLOSING ROWS ARE WRITTEN AND VALIDATED at fix = 38/38, with names checked mechanically against the gate\u0027s `^$v  .*ok$` rule (all \u003c= 12 chars, no dups, no prefix collisions): `re-report` kills E2; `xwindow` + `xwindow x1` kill E1; `dma putw` kills E3; `dma rd txt` + `dma wr txt` kill E4 at ZERO extra cost by reading existing transcripts. Gate pin 32 -\u003e 38. *** THE DIFF IS NOT RETRIEVABLE, and this note claimed it was -- corrected 2026-08-24 after looking for it. *** It lived in the measuring seat\u0027s OWN session scratchpad, a different path from the main loop\u0027s, and that directory is gone. A CLAIM THAT AN ARTEFACT IS RETAINED IS A CLAIM A FUTURE ROUND WILL ACT ON, so it has to be checked before it is written -- the same class as a score claim naming a log that does not exist. What survives is the DESIGN, which is recorded above in full and is enough to rebuild from: `re-report` kills E2 (3 != 2); `xwindow` + `xwindow x1` kill E1 (0x11223345 != 0x11223344); `dma putw` kills E3 (0xffffffff != 0x11223344); `dma rd txt` + `dma wr txt` kill E4 (0 != 1 each) at zero extra cost by reading existing transcripts. Rebuilding is a short job; hunting the file is not. ALSO MEASURED EQUIVALENT (not escapes, worth recording so nobody rebuilds them): deleting the bounds guard entirely; narrowing `size_t k` to uint8_t; deleting the zero-fill; handing the macro a pointer. And two mutants are HARD BUILD ERRORS -- widening or narrowing the latch element type -- which is what refutes this round\u0027s own \u0027the obvious sanity check passes\u0027 warning."
                        }
                    ]
    },
