    {
        "id":  "gdromdmaclear",
        "state":  "open",
        "commits":  "",
        "hold":  "",
        "witness":  "none -- coverage debt above the vacuity floor",
        "title":  "gdromdmaclear  a decline path that CLEARS an already-pending DMA completion escapes all 52 rows -- nodata quiet pins one direction of a two-way transition",
        "closure":  {
                        "by":  "",
                        "badge":  "",
                        "note":  ""
                    },
        "entries":  [
                        {
                            "seat":  "fable5",
                            "phase":  "assess",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "note":  "THE ONE ESCAPE THE CAPPED ASK-AGAIN FOUND AND KEPT. On a decline path, clear an already-pending GD-ROM DMA completion (plausible as \u0027failure cleanup\u0027). `nodata quiet` cannot see it: that row proves a decline does not SET the bit, and M8 measured that transition (clear-\u003eset); this is set-\u003eclear, and every session() forks a fresh emulator so nothing is pending when the row runs. A ROW CAN PIN ONE DIRECTION OF A TWO-WAY TRANSITION AND READ AS THOUGH IT PINNED BOTH. The proposed construction does NOT work as written -- the success arm does not free d-\u003edata, so a second trigger succeeds again rather than reaching the no-data arm. The CHEAP variant is the ARMING arm: after a successful DMA write +0x0c=0 to break the arming, trigger, and require the completion bit STILL SET. Three stores on a session that exists. FILED not fixed: the detector is above the floor, and a row added at that point would ship WITHOUT a measured kill -- adding an unmeasured row to satisfy a rule about rows is the vacuity the rule exists to prevent."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "codex",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "note":  "SURVIVING MUTANT, and it is the one this row exists for: clear an already-pending GD-ROM DMA completion on a decline path. Found in panel_20260824_055023, the round\u0027s single capped ask-again. SURVIVING MUTANT: clear an already-pending GD-ROM DMA completion on a decline path. `nodata quiet` cannot see it -- that row pins one DIRECTION of a two-way transition (clear-\u003eset, which M8 measured); this is set-\u003eclear, and every session forks a fresh emulator so nothing is pending when the row runs."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "agy",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "note":  "PANEL panel_20260824_055023. GO-WITH-CHANGES, and it named THREE uncaught mutants in an answer I recorded from its verdict line and did not open -- the same defect as the sibling seat whose unread answer held a real L2 breach. (1) CROSS-WINDOW LATCH ALIAS: use the CONTROL window\u0027s GDROM_W_NODATA (0x02) in place of GDROM_W_DMANODATA (0x10). `both decl` cannot see it because no session drives both windows\u0027 warn bits -- ALREADY COVERED by the open gdromwarnbits row, so not re-filed. (2) M_trig_zero: `if (idata != 0)` -\u003e `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)`, so a STORE OF ZERO enters the block. VERIFIED REAL: no row stores 0 to +0x18 anywhere. It also refutes an earlier seat\u0027s parenthetical that `dma accept` writes zero -- that row READS five offsets with r2=0 as a poison preset. Distinct from gdromdmasilent (`idata == 1`, which makes values \u003e= 2 skip): together they show the HEAD GUARD is undertested from BOTH sides, no row driving it with 0 or with \u003e= 2. (3) M_relax_arm: `(count \u0026 1) \u0026\u0026 (enable \u0026 1)`. VERIFIED INVISIBLE at every point any row drives -- (0,0), (1,0), (1,1), (2,1), (1,2) all agree with the shipped form; it first differs at (3,1), and no row drives count=3. That is a THIRD member of the arming-widening family alongside gdromdmaguard\u0027s `\u003c=` and the measured M10 truthiness form. FILED as coverage debt: the round\u0027s L2 breach (nodata twice) is already in flight, and these are above-floor."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "grok",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "note":  "PANEL panel_20260824_055023. *** FOUND A REAL ESCAPE, AND IT SAT UNREAD FOR HOURS BECAUSE I RECORDED ITS VERDICT LINE AND NEVER OPENED THE 6,732 BYTES BEHIND IT. *** The mutant: in the `d-\u003edata == NULL` arm, move `idata = 0` INSIDE the `if (!(d-\u003ewarned \u0026 GDROM_W_DMANODATA))` block -- what a maintainer tidying that block, or believing \u0027ack the start only when we actually complain\u0027, would write. The FIRST no-data trigger still prints and still clears; every later one leaves the guest\u0027s trigger value in dma_reg[6] and +0x18 reads BUSY FOREVER. THE SEAT NAMED WHY NO ROW CATCHES IT, EXACTLY: \u0027That is the exact shape `arm twice` was written to close. The row is arming-only. There is no `nodata twice`.\u0027 VERIFIED by parsing every session in the probe: the sessions reaching the NO-DATA arm each trigger it ONCE (their second +0x18 reference is the readback, not a second trigger), and `both decl` drives each arm once rather than one arm twice. Under the CORRECTED clause (d) -- which enumerates predicates the fix DEPENDS ON, not only those it adds -- this is guard-kept-repair-nulled, an L2 floor breach. THE GATE\u0027S WARNING WAS LITERAL: \u0027a seat that answered and was not recorded is an UNREAD REVIEW, and this project has twice found real defects sitting in one.\u0027 This is the third."
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "kimi",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "note":  "PANEL panel_20260824_055023. Named a vacuity class the other seats did not: *** A ROW WHOSE ONLY KILL IS A LETHAL MUTANT IS NOT PROVEN TO OBSERVE WHAT IT CLAIMS. *** Under a host-death mutant EVERY row reads \u0027died\u0027, so a red there says nothing about the row\u0027s own subject. It nominated two rows; checking both against the data settled them in OPPOSITE directions. (1) `nodata notx` -- REFUTED. It does have a non-lethal kill: the PRE-MOVE build (probe_pre51.raw), where `arm survive` and `nodata live` both read `survived` (host alive) while `nodata notx` reads 1 against 0. The seat could not have known -- that measurement is not in the kill table the brief inlined, which is a defect in MY brief rather than in its reasoning. (2) `arm says` -- HOLDS. Its only kill in the table is Mb2, which is lethal, and on the pre-move build it reads 1 against 1, green. So the row claims \u0027the decline SPEAKS\u0027 and has NEVER been shown to redden when the decline goes SILENT without the host dying. The closing mutant is cheap and obvious once named: delete the `fatal()` from the arming arm, keep the latch and keep `idata = 0` -- host survives, no diagnostic, `arm says` reads 0 against 1. FILED as coverage debt, above floor. *THE CLASS GENERALISES BEYOND THIS PROBE: any detector whose mutants are mostly lethal will show rows that look validated and are not, and the discriminator is whether a row has a kill that leaves the host ALIVE.*"
                        },
                        {
                            "seat":  "deepseek",
                            "phase":  "review",
                            "d":  "�o"",
                            "date":  "2026-08-24",
                            "note":  "Proposed moving `idata = 0` to the START of the success arm (panel_20260824_055023, the capped ask-again). REFUTED BY READING: Proposed moving `idata = 0` to the START of the success arm. REFUTED by reading: the transfer is atomic within the guest\u0027s own store, so there is no \u0027during\u0027 for a guest to observe. A seat wrong for an interesting reason -- it reasoned about a hardware timing model this emulator does not have."
                        },
                        {
