gdromkeyindex
18 builds, 20 probe runs, against source byte-identical to ba30e64. FIVE new 32/32 escapes. FILED not fixed: the stopping rule reserves in-round fixes for a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD, and #461's detector is above the vacuity floor on all four clauses -- so these are coverage debt, the #447 disposition. *** E1 IS THE FINDING, AND IT DEFEATS THE ROUND'S STRUCTURAL DEFENCE BY GOING ROUND IT. *** Exchange the word- and bit-index expressions in gdrom_unimpl_first (`bits[k >> 5]` / `1u << (k & 31)` transposed). MEASURED, twice, with a standalone probe: four DECLINED 32-bit READS in the CONTROL window write 1 into dma_reg[1] (destination), [2] (length), [3] (count) and [5] (enable) -- which is EXACTLY case 0x18's arming condition, reached from the wrong window entirely. bits is a uint32_t[2]; `bits[k & 31]` runs to index 31 and the struct puts dma_reg[0] six words past it. ASan is blind (member-to-member inside one malloc). *** THE BOUNDS GUARD STILL PASSES: k is still off/4 < nkeys. The round's defence protects the BOUND; E1 lives in the INDEX, and no row was on the index. *** Honest scope from the seat: it is one transposition, and each HALF dies alone (m_swapidx 31/32, m_swapbit 29/32) -- the rows measure key DISTINCTNESS, which a transposition preserves exactly, so the detector catches either half and misses the whole. E2 `|=` -> `=` (32/32): defeats once-per-offset on any REVISIT -- read +0x00, +0x04, +0x00 gives three lines, so a repeated register scan re-floods forever. Every existing row is blind because none revisits a word after touching another in the same bitmap word. E3 the alignment guard rewritten as `len != sizeof(uint32_t)` (32/32): re-opens the alias, MEASURED -- a 4-byte write at DMA+0x05 lands 0xffffffff in dma_reg[1] against the seeded 0x11223344. Every existing guard row uses a NARROW access, so width and alignment are indistinguishable to the detector. No SH4 instruction can issue a misaligned 32-bit store, but memory_rw can, and debugger_cmds.c:1353 is one caller. E4 both alignment ternaries in the DMA diagnostics inverted (32/32) -- the messages lie about dropped-vs-stored and no row reads the text. E5 `if (idata != 0)` -> `if (0)` at case 0x18 (32/32): the DMA arm can be present, correctly routed, and completely INERT. Outside #461's semantic class -- it belongs to whoever fixes gdromexitsix's DMA+0x18 pair, and it is the same gap kimi named as its M2. SIX CLOSING ROWS ARE WRITTEN AND VALIDATED at fix = 38/38, with names checked mechanically against the gate's `^$v  .*ok$` rule (all <= 12 chars, no dups, no prefix collisions): `re-report` kills E2; `xwindow` + `xwindow x1` kill E1; `dma putw` kills E3; `dma rd txt` + `dma wr txt` kill E4 at ZERO extra cost by reading existing transcripts. Gate pin 32 -> 38. *** THE DIFF IS NOT RETRIEVABLE, and this note claimed it was -- corrected 2026-08-24 after looking for it. *** It lived in the measuring seat's OWN session scratchpad, a different path from the main loop's, and that directory is gone. A CLAIM THAT AN ARTEFACT IS RETAINED IS A CLAIM A FUTURE ROUND WILL ACT ON, so it has to be checked before it is written -- the same class as a score claim naming a log that does not exist. What survives is the DESIGN, which is recorded above in full and is enough to rebuild from: `re-report` kills E2 (3 != 2); `xwindow` + `xwindow x1` kill E1 (0x11223345 != 0x11223344); `dma putw` kills E3 (0xffffffff != 0x11223344); `dma rd txt` + `dma wr txt` kill E4 (0 != 1 each) at zero extra cost by reading existing transcripts. Rebuilding is a short job; hunting the file is not. ALSO MEASURED EQUIVALENT (not escapes, worth recording so nobody rebuilds them): deleting the bounds guard entirely; narrowing `size_t k` to uint8_t; deleting the zero-fill; handing the macro a pointer. And two mutants are HARD BUILD ERRORS -- widening or narrowing the latch element type -- which is what refutes this round's own 'the obvious sanity check passes' warning.
sh4pclockquiet
COVERAGE DEBT filed after #459's capped ask-again. A measuring seat built six mutants and FIVE scored 16/16: m_unie (`&& !(idata & TCR_UNIE)`) which lands a TCR with timer_hz == 0 for EXACTLY the value a guest must write to use a TMU at all, while going QUIETER than the fix; m_below_switch (guard moved below the debug line) giving 4 unlatched `clock set to 0.000000 Hz` announcements for 4 stores; m_valkey (key `idata ? idata : 1u`) giving 3 lines for 4 values; m_tmr2 (`&& timer_nr != 2`); and m_wrongnr (every line says 'timer 0'). Only m_valnarrow died, 15/16, killed by `unlatch`. *** THE ROOT CAUSE IS ONE SENTENCE: the declining-write value set is exactly {0x0000, 0x0001} -- the three kill rows and says/once/pertmr all write 0x0000 and unlatch writes TCR_TPSC_P16, so ANY narrowing predicate that admits both escapes. *** m_valnarrow's death shows the remedy: a second distinct value. FOUR of the five closing rows cost ZERO extra sessions; only unlatch2 at TCR_UNIE|TCR_TPSC_P4 needs a new one, and it kills m_unie and the whole narrowing family.
gatebuildallow
FOUND BY RUNNING THE DEAD-MAN SWITCH WHEN THE OWNER ASKED ABOUT CRON STATUS -- not by any
gate, and not by anyone reading the battery.  `nightly_check.sh` has been reporting
NIGHTLY_CHECK_FAIL for five days.

THE 2026-08-23 WEEKLY BATTERY WENT RED: RUN_SH_EXIT=1, VERDICT_TOKEN=REGRESS_FAIL,
passed 14 / failed 1 / skipped 1, on a DIRTY tree (`*** VERDICT DOES NOT DESCRIBE A
COMMIT ***`).  The Windows task's own LastTaskResult is 1 and has been since.

*** THE FAILING CHECK IS ONE LINE OF TEXT, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IN gate_build PASSED: ***
both trees built, 223 and 224 objects, binaries produced, zero errors, zero warnings.

    FAIL no stale allowlist entries    got=1 want=0
         STALE (listed as divergent, but the files now agree): promemul/arcbios.c

AND IT CASCADED, exactly as the carrier's serialisation note predicts: gate_build withdraws
the shared arc binary at gate start and republishes ONLY on success, so its failure left the
binary unpublished and **gate_mips SKIPPED** -- a whole gate's coverage lost, scored as SKIP
rather than as a failure.  One stale line cost two gates.

ALREADY FIXED IN THE TREE, by accident rather than by anyone chasing it: `867c697` (#481)
removed the arcbios entry.  So a re-run should be green -- but nothing re-ran it, and the
dead-man switch cannot tell 'fixed' from 'never looked at'.

*** THE DEFECT THAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS THE ONE WORTH A ROUND: gate_build.sh PINS ITS OWN
ALLOWLIST. ***  `tools/est_divergence.txt` was created precisely to stop that -- its own
header says 'This file exists because the allowlist used to be PINNED inside the checker and
went stale TWICE'.  `precommit_check.sh` reads the file.  **`gate_build.sh` does not --
`grep -n est_divergence regress/gate_build.sh` returns NOTHING** -- and its pinned copy is
what went stale.

The two disagree TODAY, measured:
    gate_build.sh DIVERGENT  (5)  devices/{Makefile.skel,autodev.c,dev_jazz.c,dev_ne2000.c}
                                  machines/machine_arc.c
    est_divergence.txt       (6)  the same five PLUS devices/.index

The `.index` gap looks benign today -- the 8/23 run reported 'no divergence outside the
documented set: 0', so `list_diffs` does not surface it -- but benign-by-luck is the state
the file was written to end, and the stale entry proves the pinned copy drifts.

FIX (not taken here, because changing a gate owes negative controls): make gate_build.sh
READ est_divergence.txt, as precommit_check.sh already does.  Controls owed in both
directions -- prove it fails on an injected stale entry, and prove it passes unchanged on
the real list.

*And a scheduling hazard worth its own sentence: the battery fires on a fixed schedule and
caught the tree MID-EDIT (five modified files, including the device being worked that
afternoon).  A verdict that does not describe a commit cannot be attributed to anything,
which is the one thing a weekly battery exists to do.*
